'''Tauranga''' is a New Zealand parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]], returning one Member of Parliament to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]]. The current MP for Tauranga is [[Sam Uffindell]] of the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]], who won the seat in the [[2022 Tauranga by-election]], following the resignation of the previous MP, [[Simon Bridges]] of the National Party.

==Population centres==
The previous electoral redistribution was undertaken in 1875 for the [[1875–1876 New Zealand general election|1875–1876 election]]. In the six years since, New Zealand's European population had increased by 65%. In the 1881 electoral redistribution, the [[New Zealand House of Representatives|House of Representatives]] increased the number of European representatives to 91 (up from 84 since the 1875–76 election). The number of Māori electorates was held at four. The House further decided that electorates should not have more than one representative, which led to 35 new electorates being formed, including Tauranga, and two electorates that had previously been abolished to be recreated. This necessitated a major disruption to existing boundaries.{{sfn|McRobie |1989 |pp=43–48}}

{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Tauranga.map|frame-longitude=176.16|frame-latitude=-37.69|zoom=10|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
The electorate includes [[Tauranga]], [[Mount Maunganui]] and [[Omanu]], but excluding Hairini, Maungatapu, Matapihi and Welcome Bay. In the 2025 boundary review, the electorate was shifted westward, gaining the communities of [[Te Puna]], [[Te Puna West]] and [[Minden, New Zealand|Minden]] while transferring [[Mount Maunganui]] and [[Omanu]] to the newly created [[Mt Maunganui (New Zealand electorate)|Mt Maunganui]] electorate.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=11 September 2025 |page=7 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref>

==History==
Tauranga electorate was created for the [[1881 New Zealand general election|1881 election]], which determined the composition of the [[8th New Zealand Parliament|8th Parliament]].  Initially, it existed until the [[1890 New Zealand general election|1890 election]] and during that time, it was represented by four MPs.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=164}}

The 1881 election was hotly contested. Four candidates were nominated: [[George Morris (New Zealand politician)|George Morris]], who had previously represented the {{NZ electorate link|East Coast}} electorate; [[George Vesey Stewart]], then the owner of the ''[[Bay of Plenty Times]]''; [[William Kelly (New Zealand politician)|William Kelly]], who had also previously represented the East Coast electorate; and Henry Thomas Rowe, a surveyor and commission agent.<ref name="1881 nomination" /> Rowe announced his retirement from the contest on 6 December three days out from election day, urging his supporters to vote for Stewart instead.<ref>{{cite news |title=The Bay of Plenty Times |date=7 December 1881 |volume=X |issue=1158 |url= https://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=BOPT18811207.2.4 |access-date=30 November 2013 |page=2}}</ref> The unofficial results were released the day after the election (Saturday, 10 December) and Morris had a majority of 13 votes over Stewart, with the official declaration to be made on 12 December.<ref>{{cite news |title=The Bay of Plenty Times |date=10 December 1881 |volume=X |issue=1161 |url= https://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=BOPT18811210.2.5 |access-date=30 November 2013 |page=2}}</ref> This was deferred until 14 December, with Morris ahead by 10 votes.<ref name="1881 results" /> Stewart stood for the [[Mayor of Tauranga|Tauranga mayoralty]] a few months later and was elected the town's first mayor.<ref>{{DNZB|Rorke|Jinty|2s45|George Vesey Stewart|1 December 2011}}</ref>

Morris was re-elected in the {{NZ election link|1884}}, but resigned in April 1885, as he had been appointed to the [[New Zealand Legislative Council|Legislative Council]].{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|pp=81, 127}}  The resulting by-election on 22 May {{By-election link year|Tauranga|1885}} was won by [[John Sheehan (New Zealand politician)|John Sheehan]], who died on 12 June 1885.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=138}}  The second {{By-election link year|Tauranga|1885}} by-election on 11 July was won by [[Lawrence Grace]], who represented the electorate until the end of the term in 1887.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=109}}  The {{NZ election link|1887}} was won by [[William Kelly (New Zealand politician)|William Kelly]], who represented the electorate until the end of the term in 1890,{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=118}} at which time the electorate was abolished.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=164}}

The electorate was recreated in 1908.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=164}} [[William Herries]] was the first representative, elected at the {{NZ election link|1908}}; he had since the {{NZ election link|1896}} represented the {{NZ electorate link|Bay of Plenty}} electorate. He became a member of the [[Reform Party (New Zealand)|Reform Party]] when it formed itself in the following year. Herries represented the electorate until his death on 22 February 1923.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=113}}

The resulting {{By-election link year|Tauranga|1923}} by-election was won by [[Charles Macmillan]], who also represented the Reform Party. Macmillan won the three subsequent general elections{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=124}} before he was beaten in the {{NZ election link|1935}} by [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]]'s [[Charles Burnett (politician)|Charles Burnett]].{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=98}} At the next election held in {{NZ election link year|1938}}, Burnett was beaten by [[New Zealand National Party|National]]'s [[Frederick Doidge]], who held the electorate until his retirement in 1951.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=103}}

Doidge was succeeded by [[George Walsh (New Zealand politician)|George Walsh]], who won the {{NZ election link|1951}}. Walsh served for seven terms and retired in 1972. [[Keith Allen (New Zealand politician)|Keith Allen]] was the next representative, first elected in {{NZ election link year|1972}} and an MP until his death shortly before the {{NZ election link|1984}}.

Allen's death did not cause a by-election, as it occurred within six months of the next general election. The 1984 election was won by [[Winston Peters]], who had previously represented the {{NZ electorate link|Hunua}} electorate. In 1990 until March 1991, Peters was [[Minister of Māori Affairs]],<ref name="Peters parliamentary bio">{{cite web|title=Rt Hon Winston Peters |url= http://www.parliament.nz/en-NZ/MPP/MPs/MPs/3/2/c/50MP13701-Peters-Winston.htm |publisher=New Zealand Parliament |access-date=28 February 2012}}</ref> but he was sacked from Cabinet by Prime Minister [[Jim Bolger]] in October 1991 after repeatedly criticising his National Party leadership.  Peters remained as a National [[backbencher]], continuing to criticise the party. In late 1992, when the National Party was considering possible candidates for the elections in the following year, it was decided that Peters would not be allowed to seek renomination for the Tauranga electorate. Peters unsuccessfully challenged this decision in the [[High Court of New Zealand|High Court]], and in early 1993, he chose to resign from the party and from Parliament. This prompted a [[1993 Tauranga by-election|by-election in Tauranga]] some months before the scheduled general election. Peters stood as an independent and won with over 90% of the vote, assisted by the major parties not standing candidates against him.<ref>{{cite news |last=Levy |first=Danya |title=Winston Peters aims to lead the opposition |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/dominion-post/news/politics/election-2011/6045338/Winston-Peters-aims-to-lead-the-opposition |access-date=28 February 2012 |work=[[The Dominion Post (Wellington)|The Dominion Post]] |date=28 November 2011}}</ref>  Shortly before the [[1993 New Zealand general election|1993 election]], Peters established [[New Zealand First]] and retained the Tauranga electorate. He continued to represent Tauranga until he was defeated in the {{NZ election link|2005}} by National's [[Bob Clarkson]].<ref name="2005 Tauranga">{{cite web|title=Official Count Results – Tauranga |url= http://electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2005/electorate-52.html |publisher=Chief Electoral Office|access-date=28 February 2012}}</ref>

Clarkson's defeat of Winston Peters was significant, as this resulted in New Zealand First losing its only electorate seat. The party still gained parliamentary representation by polling over the five percent threshold, however. Clarkson retired at the end of the parliamentary term. He was succeeded by National's [[Simon Bridges]], who won the {{NZ election link|2008}}, with Peters coming a distant second.<ref name="2008 Tauranga">{{cite web|title=Official Count Results – Tauranga |url= http://electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2008/electorate-51.html |publisher=Chief Electoral Office|access-date=28 February 2012}}</ref>  Bridges was re-elected in {{NZ election link year|2011}}.<ref name="2011 Tauranga">{{cite web |title=Official Count Results – Tauranga |url= http://electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2011/electorate-51.html |publisher=Chief Electoral Office |access-date=28 February 2012}}</ref>

